    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d478">

                <pb facs="0576" n="566" sameAs="frus:frus1947v07/pg566"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/6–2147: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-06-21T11:00:00+09:00">June 21, 1947—11
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 21—6 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1360. Paxton’s 272, June 19 states that as he considers visit without delay to
                    scene of Peitashan incident important he had that day sent <pb facs="0577"
                        n="567" xml:id="pg567"/> Mackiernan<anchor n="35" xml:id="d478-f1"/> in
                    command car with Consulate chauffeur and Chinese-Kazak interpreter in attempt to
                    reach that place. As Mackiernan speaks no Chinese, General Sung agreed send
                    along his own interpreter. As road reported impassable in spots for motor
                    vehicles, they may travel last part on horseback. Trip will probably last 10
                    days. Sung pledged to secrecy and no one else informed.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d478-f1" n="35" xml:id="d478-f1-content">
                <p>Douglas S. Mackiernan, American clerk at the Consulate at Tihwa.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
